<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  A PARENT THAT INVOKES A CHILD SESSION — the smallest thing that can answer
  whether `<invoke>` is usable in this tree.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY THIS IS ASKED BEFORE ANYTHING IS BUILT ON IT. This workspace's own
  rule is that a shape no gate has driven is not known to work here: the
  `ai_loop` document carries the same warning about `===`, `JSON.stringify` and
  template literals, every one of which the engine's NAME promises and the
  pinned engine has never been shown to do. `<invoke>` is exactly that kind of
  promise — the generator has invoke filters and the conformance suite has
  invoke tests, and both of those are facts about SOMEWHERE ELSE until a
  document in THIS crate compiles and runs one.

  What is being asked, precisely:

    1. does a document carrying `<invoke type="scxml" src="...">` compile at all
       through this crate's build script;
    2. does the child actually run;
    3. does the child's `<donedata>` arrive at the parent as `_event.data` on
       `done.invoke.<id>`;
    4. ⚠⚠⚠ CAN THE PARENT FILL THE CHILD'S DATAMODEL — `<param>` on the invoke
       reaching a `<data>` the child declared.

  Answering NO to any of them is as useful as YES: it is the difference between
  designing a sub-machine and filing an upstream ask.

  ⚠⚠⚠ WHY 4 WAS ADDED, AND WHAT RESTS ON IT. The owner's design has the template
  serving OTHER repositories and holding several loop KINDS — a debt loop, a
  feature loop — possibly running at once. Measured, what separates those kinds
  is entirely `<data>`: the consents, the standing instructions, the prompts, the
  marker, where the work list lives. **The topology is identical.** So the honest
  shape is one topology and N documents that own decisions — and the only way to
  build that WITHOUT copying a 2,000-line machine into every kind is for a parent
  to fill a child's datamodel. If it cannot, copying is back on the table, and
  this file's own workspace has already recorded what two copies of one rule cost
  when they drift.
-->
<scxml xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/07/scxml"
       version="1.0"
       name="probe_parent"
       datamodel="ecmascript"
       initial="asking">

  <datamodel>
    <data id="carried" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ WHAT THE CHILD HEARD, echoed back. This is question 4 (below), and it
      is the one a composed design rests on: a parent that owns the DECISIONS and
      a child that owns the TOPOLOGY only works if the parent can fill the
      child's datamodel. `told_it` is what was sent; the child returns it as
      `heard`, and the two being equal is the proof.
    -->
    <data id="told_it" expr="'the parent filled this in'"/>
    <data id="echoed" expr="''"/>
    <!--
      ⚠⚠⚠ THE SHAPE THE SPLIT NEEDS. Two clauses rather than one, because a list
      that lost an element and a list that arrived whole are the same length at
      one. Two FIELDS each, because an object flattened to a string still has a
      length.
    -->
    <data id="told_rules"
          expr="[
            { when: 'the first clause', text: 'what to say about it' },
            { when: 'the second clause', text: 'and about this one' }
          ]"/>
    <data id="echoed_rules" expr="[]"/>
  </datamodel>

  <state id="asking">
    <invoke type="scxml" src="probe_child.scxml" id="probe">
      <!-- ⚠ `<param>` and not `namelist`: a namelist copies variables that must
           already share a NAME across two documents, which is the coupling a
           template exists to avoid. A param names the child's slot explicitly. -->
      <param name="told" expr="told_it"/>
      <param name="rules" expr="told_rules"/>
    </invoke>
    <transition event="done.invoke.probe" target="heard">
      <assign location="carried" expr="_event.data.carried"/>
      <assign location="echoed" expr="_event.data.heard"/>
      <assign location="echoed_rules" expr="_event.data.heard_rules"/>
    </transition>
  </state>

  <final id="heard"/>
</scxml>
